# Task 02 — Bento Grid Dashboard

**Category:** Layout & Structure
**Difficulty:** advanced
**Style school:** Material Design 3

## The problem

A dashboard with unevenly sized cells (a Bento-style grid) that stays visually
organized instead of chaotic, with one larger cell highlighting the most
important stat.

## Constraints

- All cell sizes are set with `grid-column`/`grid-row` spans only — no fixed
  pixel widths or heights anywhere.
- The layout must look intentionally different at three sizes (mobile,
  tablet, desktop), not just "not broken."

## Key implementation decisions

- Three explicit breakpoints (`base`, `md`, `lg`) each get their own
  `col-span`/`row-span` values per card, producing a genuinely different
  arrangement at each size instead of one layout that just reflows.
- `grid-rows-3` (explicit, fixed row count) is only applied from `md:` up.
  On mobile the grid uses `grid-rows-none` so the browser generates implicit
  rows sized to each card's natural content height — with an explicit row
  count, the 5th card (the wide summary panel) would get squeezed into a
  shared row with "Bounce Rate" and inherit its shorter height instead of
  its own.
- Colors come entirely from the `brand-*` / `neutral-*` token scale — no
  default Tailwind palette colors used.

